questions.question:
This metaclass should make every method whose name starts with must_ abstract. It enforces Worker correctly but wrongly keeps RealWorker abstract. Fix the bug.
from abc import ABCMeta
class NameMeta(ABCMeta):
    def __new__(mcls, name, bases, ns):
        cls = super().__new__(mcls, name, bases, ns)
        abstract = set()
        for key, value in ns.items():
            if key.startswith('must_'):
                abstract.add(key)
        cls.__abstractmethods__ = frozenset(abstract)
        return cls
class Worker(metaclass=NameMeta):
    def must_work(self): pass
class RealWorker(Worker):
    def must_work(self): return 'done'

questions.answer:
Fix the loop: it keeps re-marking must_* methods even when this class is the one implementing them. Start from inherited abstract sets and remove names overridden here.

code:
from abc import ABCMeta

class NameMeta(ABCMeta):
    def __new__(mcls, name, bases, ns):
        abstract = set()
        for base in bases:
            abstract |= set(getattr(base, '__abstractmethods__', ()))
        for key in ns:
            if key in abstract:
                abstract.remove(key)
            elif key.startswith('must_') and not callable(ns[key]):
                abstract.add(key)
        cls = super().__new__(mcls, name, bases, ns)
        cls.__abstractmethods__ = frozenset(abstract)
        return cls

class Worker(metaclass=NameMeta):
    def must_work(self): pass

class RealWorker(Worker):
    def must_work(self):
        return 'done'

print(RealWorker().must_work())  # done

real example:
Meta-abstract rules must respect which class actually supplies the method.

